Yes, yet another simple valkyrie ascension. :)

I don't quite remember the beginnings of the game, since it was quite a
long time since I have started it. I remember finding a bones file some-
where which wielded me a silver dragon scale mail along with some other
goodies.

This was my first (!) game where I actually found Ludios.  On the other
hand, this is my third game to get past the castle (and my second ascen-
sion), so the rate might not be so intriguing. :)

Well, the first source of wishing I found was the castle wand. With its
help, I did conjur up missing parts of my armory: a pair of speed boots
and a T-shirt was what I wished for,  besides the two standard charging
scrolls and a blessed magic marker.

After mopping up the Castle, I did end up roaming up Sokoban as a small
distraction, only to leave the last level unattended;  it was the level
with the bag of holding, and alas, I already had one. Also, I was in po-
ssession of a source of reflection, so I wouldn't have done otherwise.

After that, I did the Quest. Yes, I did the Quest after the Castle. Why,
some might ask: well, I'm usually not at all the right level for having
the quest at the time when I come across the portal; in this game, this
was also the case, me being a meagerly level 12 valkyrie. Proved not to
be a challenge, as I was already a walking dreadnaught by that time. My
nemesis fell like any other fire giant. I gathered up the loot and went
back into the dungeon.

Upon having an ID fest near an altar, however, I managed to do a Unwise
Thing[tm]: in a hurry, I accidentally put the unidentified brass wand I
feared to be cancellation in the bag of holding. Poof. A various assort-
ment of gems, scrolls, potions, etc. pp., all blown into nonexistance.

I was at the point of just walking out the dungeon and escaping at that
very moment, cursing myself for me idiocy.

Well, needless to say, I obviously decided to do so otherwise, and went
up to Sokoban to fetch that spare BoH; after all, I was still alive and
had a quite acceptable equipment (armor plus protection summing up to a
total AC of -31, as well as +5/+6-enchanted Frost Brand and a silver sa-
bre), and after that, proceeded on to gather various equipment; ransack-
ing previous levels, I gather up potions, cancelled unneeded ones as to
get water, replicated holy water [side note: I prayed two times in this
game, and just for water.], gathered up scrolls, and the likes.

In the end, noticing a lack of identify scrolls anywhere in between the
Valley and topside, I started scavenging spellbooks and, quite the litt-
le intelligent (13) beast my valkyrie was, read them all, save that sil-
ly finger of death book which only gave her headaches. Some time around,
I managed to find a identify book.  I grabbed the robe of a late priest
who had the misfortune of being cross-aligned and attending the only al-
tar besides minetown, and retreated myself into a closet to start iding
the whole of my equipment. The failure rate of 53% was quite impressive.

That settled, fully bolstered up, I went back to minetown and butchered
the local priest, giving me back my $BIGNUM zorkmids from Ludios I acci-
dentally donated all at once, and went to the valley to beef me up. All
set and done: AC -40, get 'em coming. Exeunt ad gehennem.

Gehennom was boring as usual. Every major demon lord I encountered fell
within two or three turns of double-wielding madness (no, not you, Mark)
and proved no challenge. Only fun I had during that was Orcus, silly as
he is drinking a potion of gain level which sent him flying through the
ceiling. =)

Had a bit of a trouble finding Vlad,  noticing that I already should've
the entrance to his tower after noticing a kind of strange vibration un-
der my feet. Marked it with a candle, and went back up. I did have a te-
dious search there, as his upstairs were obviously covered by some item.
Finally found him, went in there, and zapped my wand of lighting in his
general direction. After doing that, I eventually moved out of the litt-
le stair room holding the stairs, and into his throne room, to actually
kill that pest, and not zap high voltages at a poor and innocent wall.

Bell of Opening and Candelabrum of Invocation obtained: off to kill the
Wizard.

Proved to be another cakewalk. Went through the bee-hive acquiring some
royal jelly on the way. The zoo was managed in a relaxing way by merely
putting on a ring of conflict and kicking the door. The only thing that
was sorely missed was the popcorn.

Another level up, digged into the wall of his abode and obliterated him
with a trusty wand of death. Tinned the corpse, fetched the Book. Going
down. [This is where I saved yesterday; today's episode, as observed by
#nethack on freenode, showed the following episodes.]

Well, long story short, nothing interesting happened in the Sanctuum. I
once stripped to fireproof my speed boots as I burned them on those pes-
ky fire traps surrounding the Inner Sanctuum. Went on the run out of Ge-
hennom, being VERY annoyed by the Mysterious Fa^Horce[tm] on the way.

Killed the wizard a whopping eight times before Earth, but nothing else.
Earth, air, fire and water were easily managed with the help of the Orb
of Fate; Air once required healing due to some air elementals.

Arriving on Astral, I was assigned my Guardian Angel of Tyr, and thusly
went to search for my instrument of ascension on the left-hand side, ha-
ving mearly Death as its guardian; I went on with complicated foils, as
to protect myself from dangers, using cursed scrolls of summon monsters
to keep priests away. It proved quite senseless, really, as I never had
the chance to drop below 150 hit points (180 being my max). It then had
proved to be the wrong altar, anyway. So I went back and Pestilence him-
self opened the door to the middle altar way. Of course, I stepped in.

I wasn't so interested in playing it safe and just went straight to the
altar, this time. I was a bit cautious around the priests, but that was
again overcautiousness sneaking it; they did no harm, actually even hel-
ped me by summoning some weak insects (cue Shodan saying `insect').  Of
course, the middle alter was chaotic and dedicated to Loki.

Grumble, grumble.

Getting a tiny bit unsettled, I unleased the glorious fury of my little
Norse nightmare, and hacked my way to the altar, leaving behind a trail
of corpses. The Guardian Angel of Tyr died in a quite embarrassing way:
he was devoured by a trapper when I was reducing the altar guardians to
tiny heaps. I thusly reached the altar, and offered the Amulet to Tyr.

An invisible choir sung, I was bathed in radiance, lada.

